92186265641 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265641 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265641 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265641 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265641 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186264702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265641 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e12d11e5-1bfe-5244-a9f3-13036e9bc00f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263706 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186264025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265655 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e73fb32c-a004-5fbe-9c18-83ea40a2c949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265732 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265732 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265732 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265732 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3}=3 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265732 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bdb97edb-fdd8-52f8-9de8-da5dc252cf96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265732 #edb/ref 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6(-\\frac{1}{3})=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=3-2=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+1-(-\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2-1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}$, ${x}_{2}=1$, and ${x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265733 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265733 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265733 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265733 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3} & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265733 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8c3fa01d-ccbd-5a15-bdb2-2085c84ec955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265733 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say,  ${x}_{1}$, ${x}_{2}$, and ${x}_{3}.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+\\frac{{x}_{3}}{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1-\\frac{2}{3}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3(-\\frac{5}{3})+(2)=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=2+5-2=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=5$, ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{3}$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨5,-\\frac{5}{3},2⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265751 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265751 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265751 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265751 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265751 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "767893ce-f15e-5dc3-ac2a-90c7bba1c030" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265751 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 4x+y+2z=-3 \\\\  & y+3z=0 \\\\  & -z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=1\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+3(-1)=0\\,⟹\\,y=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n4x+(3)+2(-1)=-3\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{-3-3+2}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-1$, $y=3$, and $z=-1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as \n$[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265753 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265753 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129615" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265753 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265753 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & -{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=7 \\\\  & 2{x}_{3}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265753 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86f65392-52cb-532d-9e01-57782fdc15f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265753 #edb/ref 1074 "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n2{x}_{3}=4\\,⟹\\,{x}_{3}=2.\n$$\n\n Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-{x}_{2}+3(2)=7\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=6-7=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+5(-1)+(2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-1+5-2=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n${x}_{1}=2$, ${x}_{2}=-1$, and ${x}_{3}=2.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265803 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265803 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265803 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265803 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ compute $P(X=5).$  Round your final answer to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265803 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e412f016-1827-5a09-955d-1fa10d7d3776" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265803 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(0.6)}^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & ={(0.6)}^{4}(0.4) \\\\  & ≈0.0518.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265851 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265851 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130196" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265851 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265851 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15),$ compute $P(X<3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265851 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28c8d216-a346-5e8a-b9da-a29b8df87b52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265851 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.15),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(0.85)}^{x-1}(0.15), & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<3) & =P(X\\in {1,2}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2) \\\\  & ={(0.85)}^{0}(0.15)+{(0.85)}^{1}(0.15) \\\\  & =0.2775.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265858 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265858 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265858 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265858 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265858 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48e417b8-5121-57e7-8423-7df9a5c6b9ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265858 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{5}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{5}), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 5.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{5})}^{0}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{5})+{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{3}(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =0.9744.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 5) & =1-P(X<5) \\\\  & =1-0.9744 \\\\  & =0.0256.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265859 #edb/ref 1071 "Given $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{3}),$ compute $P(X\\ge 6)$ rounded to $4$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c187994c-c002-555b-aa54-6ed7098c1e28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265859 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X∼\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Geom}(\\frac{2}{3}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x-1}(\\frac{2}{3}), & x=1,2,3… \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 6.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =1-P(X<6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3)+P(X=4)+P(X=5) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{1}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}(\\frac{2}{3})+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{4}(\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.9959.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6) & =1-P(X<6) \\\\  & ≈1-0.9959 \\\\  & =0.0041.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265909 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265909 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265909 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265909 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $6\\times 6$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $A(BC)=(AB)C$\n- $A(B-C)=AB-CA$\n- $CA=AC$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265909 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da216097-76ab-5a2e-b3ad-662534506edb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265909 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $A(BC)=(AB)C$ is always true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $A(B-C)=AB-AC,$ but $AC\\ne CA$ because matrix multiplication is not commutative in general.\n\n- Statement III  is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative, so $CA\\ne AC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement  I is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265923 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265923 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265923 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265923 #edb/ref 1071 "Represent the following system of equations using matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-1=4y \\\\ y=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265923 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d99d7426-520b-5e1d-945d-430bc4355051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265923 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we organize the system so that it’s in the correct order and  both $x$ and $y$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3x-4y=1 \\\\ 0x+y=-4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265976 #edb/ref 1071 "A card is being randomly picked from a standard $52$ cards deck and put back in until getting a red card. What is the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b7df661-fc21-5c59-a0c6-d0ca3d192816" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265976 #edb/ref 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of cards picked until a red one is chosen. Since there are $26$ red cards in a $52$ cards deck, the chance of choosing a red card is $\\frac{26}{52}.$ Thus, the number of chosen cards before getting a red card can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=\\frac{26}{52}=\\frac{1}{2}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.5).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.5)^{x-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{x-1}(0.5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a red card within the first $5$ tries is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4,5}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)+f(5) \\\\  & =(0.5)^{1-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{2-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4-1}(0.5)+(0.5)^{5-1}(0.5) \\\\  & =(0.5)+(0.5)(0.5)+(0.5)^{2}(0.5)+(0.5)^{3}(0.5)+(0.5)^{4}(0.5) \\\\  & =[1+0.5+(0.5)^{2}+(0.5)^{3}+(0.5)^{4}](0.5) \\\\  & ≈0.969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266004 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266004 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266004 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266004 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $\\text{Re}(k)$ given that\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$\\overset{―}{kv}=[\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efbde7ce-c5dc-58bb-af77-efbf61eef9d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266004 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\overset{―}{kv}=\\overset{―}{v},$ we get the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{kv} & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{v} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,\\overset{―}{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{4} \\\\ \\overset{―}{-5-\\text{i}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{―}{k}\\,[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ 18-40\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{k} \\\\ (-5+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{k}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{―}{k}=-20+28\\text{i} \\\\ (-5+\\text{i})\\overset{―}{k}=18-40\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{k}=-5+7\\text{i}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain the true statement\n\n$$\n(-5+\\text{i})(-5+7\\text{i})=18-40\\text{i}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\overset{―}{-5+7\\text{i}}=-5-7\\text{i}.$\n\nFinally, $\\text{Re}(k)=-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266012 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266012 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130923" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266012 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266012 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=-1 \\\\ -4x+8y=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266012 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df63ef93-068d-5156-97ea-60671ff0c81c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266012 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we set up the augmented matrix for the system:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nReducing $M$ to row echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=-1 \\\\ 0x+0y=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the final equation $0x+0y=3$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=3,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266042 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266042 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266042 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266042 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2, & x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]$\n\nIII. $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266042 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d91387a3-da34-5d28-af8d-36338cffabf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266042 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- Consider $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}2=2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=0$ are not the same. Hence, $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=0.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Consider $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}].$ In this interval, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ so the function is not defined at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous on the interval $[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}],$ and the extreme value theorem is not applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266096 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266096 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131519" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266096 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266096 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266096 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4148a2a9-e5f7-5051-aebb-c06d4fe49aad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266096 #edb/ref 1074 "The system corresponding to $M$ is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+6y-7z=4 \\\\ 0x+3y+0z=9 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-8,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266097 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266097 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131521" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266097 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266097 #edb/ref 1071 "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-1.99<Z<-0.99).$\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0192\\,$ | $\\,0.0188\\,$ | $\\,0.0183\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0244\\,$ | $\\,0.0239\\,$ | $\\,0.0233\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0307\\,$ | $\\,0.0301\\,$ | $\\,0.0294\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0192\\,$ | $\\,0.0188\\,$ | $\\,0.0183\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0244\\,$ | $\\,0.0239\\,$ | $\\,0.0233\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0307\\,$ | $\\,0.0301\\,$ | $\\,0.0294\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ | $\\,0.1401\\,$ | $\\,0.1379\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ | $\\,0.1611\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ | $\\,0.1867\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266097 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26bc4f4b-9904-57fc-b211-497a209ba8d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266097 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-1.99<Z<-0.99).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8dc66c8ceed4366688dc040104eaece3d251ecb9f72d9aae0827ead3fe933570.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.99<Z<-0.99) & =P(Z<-0.99)-P(Z\\le -1.99) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.99)-P(Z\\le -1.99) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.99)-Φ(-1.99).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.99) & =0.0233 \\\\ Φ(-0.99) & =0.1611\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.99<Z<-0.99) & =Φ(-0.99)-Φ(-1.99) \\\\  & =0.1611-0.0233 \\\\  & =0.1378.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266113 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266113 #edb/ref 1069 "q-131719" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266113 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266113 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x+8y=-2 \\\\  & -3x-12y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266113 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7e2bc11-b1bf-5938-9267-7923a80ec463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266113 #edb/ref 1074 "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266164 #edb/ref 1080 "256-128x-64{x}^{2}+⋯" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266164 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266164 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0284f84c-f34c-5607-8e55-7544c6b2c95b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266237 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{8}-8{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266237 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266237 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a64923f-e9ca-5125-ac6a-140a5b5076d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266275 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266275 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266275 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f92a789-d109-50a4-95bb-321fbf167553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266299 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266299 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266299 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12389f18-052b-519d-9a2a-b2d04cff0ce4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266309 #edb/ref 1080 "256-256x+256{x}^{2}+⋯" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266309 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266309 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12e978b7-4b66-5dc5-8101-2e3babbf88dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266335 #edb/ref 1080 "6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266335 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266335 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1734a15f-874d-52cb-8d30-537856adcf27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266568 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{8}-7{x}^{7}y+28{x}^{6}{y}^{2}+⋯" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266568 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266568 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "302e6a79-c731-5c9b-9923-3a042c55b509" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266573 #edb/ref 1080 "8.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [